\itemize{
\item \code{rcogmod_lba2()}: Simulates random draws from the LBA.
\item \code{dcogmod_lba2()}: Computes the density (likelihood).
\item \code{cogmod_lba2()}: Creates a \code{brms::custom_family()} for use in \code{brms} models.
\item \code{cogmod_lba2_stanvars()}: Generates the \code{stanvars} to pass to \code{brm()}.
\item \code{p_outlier()}: Per-trial posterior probability of being an outlier.
}
}
\section{Parameterization}{
Accumulator \code{k} starts at a point \code{z ~ Uniform(0, sigmabias)}, drawn afresh on
every trial, and rises at a constant rate \code{v ~ Normal(drift_k, sigma_k)}, also
drawn afresh on every trial, until it reaches the threshold
\code{b = boundary + sigmabias}. Its finishing time is therefore \code{(b - z) / v}, and
\code{boundary} is the threshold \emph{offset}: the distance from the highest possible
starting point to the threshold. All the randomness is between trials; within
a trial the path is a straight line, which is what makes the density
closed-form.

This is the \code{B} parameterization of \code{DMC} and \code{EMC2}, where \code{b = B + A} with
\code{A} the start-point range. It is used for a reason rather than for taste: the
threshold has to sit above the highest possible starting point, and writing
the \emph{offset} makes \code{b > A} hold automatically for any positive value. The
alternative - estimating the absolute threshold, as \code{rtdists} does - needs an
order constraint between two estimated parameters, which has to hold in every
cell of the design once either of them carries a predictor. The cost is that
\code{boundary} alone is not the quantity to read off a fitted model; \code{boundary + sigmabias} is.

\code{ndt} is expressed \strong{directly, in seconds} (through a log link in the \code{brms}
family). Nothing about it is taken from the data: it is not bounded by the
fastest observed response, so a non-decision time that varies by condition or
by participant can exceed the sample minimum wherever the data support it.

Tying \code{ndt} to the fastest observed response would cap it at an order
statistic of the sample, so any condition or participant whose true \code{ndt}
exceeded that response would be inexpressible, and the misfit would surface
as spurious effects on the race parameters. Expressing it directly is what
avoids that.
}

\section{Negative drift rates}{
A Normal drift rate can come out negative, and such an accumulator rises
away from the threshold and never responds. Brown and Heathcote (2008) noted
the problem and left it; every implementation since has had to decide what
to do about it, and this one follows the convention of \code{rtdists}
(\code{posdrift = TRUE}, its default), \code{DMC}, \code{EMC2} and \code{ggdmc}: \strong{each drift
rate is a Normal truncated at zero}. Every accumulator is then guaranteed a
positive rate on every trial, every trial produces a response, and the
losing accumulator's survival is that of a truncated Normal.
\code{rcogmod_lba2()} draws each drift from exactly that truncated distribution.

The density is normalised to match: the winner's defective density is
divided by its own \code{pnorm(drift / sigma)}, and the loser's survival is taken
conditional on its own drift being positive. Without the truncation the
density integrates to the probability that at least one accumulator finishes
rather than to one, which at low drift rates is a long way short - \code{0.83}
at drifts of \code{0.5} and \code{0.2} with SDs of \code{1.5}, so the likelihood would be
wrong by 17\% and wrong by \emph{different} amounts at different parameter values,
which is what biases estimates rather than merely offsetting them.

Two consequences are worth knowing. First, because the convention is the
field's, parameter estimates are \strong{directly comparable} with those packages'
and with the published LBA literature built on them, and \code{\link[=dcogmod_lba2]{dcogmod_lba2()}}
reproduces \code{rtdists::dLBA()} at the same parameter values. Second, \code{drift}
and \code{sigma} are the location and scale of the \emph{untruncated} Normal, not the
mean and SD of the drifts actually realised: where a drift is small relative
to its SD, the realised mean is higher and the realised SD lower than the
parameters say. That is the price every implementation pays for a race that
always finishes; with both drifts large relative to their SDs it is
negligible.

The truncation also creates a flat direction. A Normal truncated at zero
whose location runs off to minus infinity while its scale grows, with
\verb{|drift| / sigma^2} held fixed, converges to an Exponential, so once an
accumulator rarely wins its \code{drift} and \code{sigma} are identified only through
that ratio, and the likelihood is nearly flat along the ray. The error
accumulator in a task with a 5\% error rate is exactly such a case: left
flat, its drift wandered to \code{-12} with an interval of \code{-23} to \code{-6.5}, on an
evidence scale where the correct accumulator's drift is \code{3}.
\code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} therefore puts \code{normal(1, 2)} on \code{driftone} and
\code{normal(0, 1.5)} on its slopes, as it does for \code{\link[=cogmod_lnr]{cogmod_lnr()}}'s \code{nuone}. If
the \emph{rarely} chosen option is the one on \code{mu}, mirror that prior onto \code{mu}
by hand. Fixing both SDs (\verb{sigmazero = 1, sigmaone = 1}, a single \code{sv})
removes the ray altogether and is common practice in the LBA literature.
}

\section{The evidence scale is arbitrary}{
Multiply \code{driftzero}, \code{driftone}, \code{sigmazero}, \code{sigmaone}, \code{sigmabias} and
\code{boundary} all by any \code{c > 0} and every finishing time \code{(b - z) / v} is
unchanged. The likelihood is therefore \strong{exactly} constant along that ray,
which runs to infinity in both directions: the six parameters are identified
only up to a common scale factor.

\code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} puts priors on all four positive parameters, which makes
the posterior proper and the sampler well behaved, but a prior does not
\emph{identify} a direction the likelihood cannot see. If the individual
parameters are to be interpreted - rather than the RT distribution they
jointly generate, which is perfectly well identified - fix one SD in the
formula, the usual convention being \code{sigmazero = 1}:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{f <- brms::bf(RT | dec(Error) ~ Condition, driftone ~ Condition,
              sigmazero = 1, sigmaone ~ 1, sigmabias ~ 1, boundary ~ 1,
              ndt ~ 1, poutlier ~ 1, family = cogmod_lba2())
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

A second, milder flat direction remains either way: \code{sigmabias} and
\code{boundary} enter only through the sum \code{b = boundary + sigmabias}, so they
trade off along a ridge. The sum is the trustworthy quantity to interpret and
to compare across conditions. \code{\link[=cogmod_lba1]{cogmod_lba1()}} and \code{\link[=cogmod_rdm]{cogmod_rdm()}} share it.
}

\section{The outlier component}{
A shifted distribution assigns exactly zero density to any response faster
than \code{ndt}, which puts a hard boundary in the likelihood at the fastest
observed RT. Mixing in a component with support over the whole positive line
removes it: every response keeps positive density whatever \code{ndt} is, so the
boundary becomes a finite cost rather than a wall and the log-density stays
smooth and differentiable.

Because this model produces a \strong{choice as well as a time}, the contaminant
has to produce both. It is a guess: the choice is uniform over the two
options, and the RT is a \strong{half Normal} with scale \code{0.2} seconds.

\deqn{f(t, k) = p \frac{1}{K} g(t) + (1 - p) f_k(t - ndt)}

The \code{1 / K} is what keeps the total summing to one over the response options;
without it it would come to \code{1 + poutlier}.

\code{poutlier} is a \emph{rate}, not a classification: the model never labels
individual trials, it estimates what share of them came from elsewhere. Use
\code{\link[=p_outlier]{p_outlier()}} for per-trial posterior probabilities.
}

\section{Reaction times must be in seconds}{
The outlier component's scale is a constant in seconds, and so are the
priors \code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} supplies. There is no argument for changing the
unit. Millisecond data fails silently rather than loudly - the outlier
component contributes nothing and the min-RT boundary comes back. See the
corresponding section of \code{\link[=cogmod_lognormal]{cogmod_lognormal()}} for the full account, which
applies unchanged here.
}

\section{Fitting}{
\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{f <- brms::bf(RT | dec(Error) ~ Condition, driftone ~ Condition,
              sigmazero = 1, sigmaone ~ 1, sigmabias ~ 1, boundary ~ 1,
              ndt ~ 1, poutlier ~ 1, family = cogmod_lba2())
brms::brm(f, data = df,
          prior    = cogmod_priors(f, df),
          init     = cogmod_inits(f, df),
          stanvars = cogmod_stanvars(f))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The \code{brms} family names the drift of the first accumulator \code{mu} (as \code{brms}
requires) and that of the second \code{driftone}. Use \code{\link[=cogmod_inits]{cogmod_inits()}} rather than
\code{init = 0}: \code{brms} initialises on the unconstrained scale, so \code{init = 0} puts
\code{ndt} at \code{exp(0) = 1} second - above nearly every sub-second RT, which leaves
every response attributed to the outlier component and the race parameters
with no gradient at all.
}

\section{Predictions exclude the outlier component}{
\code{posterior_predict()} describes the \strong{race alone} by default, as if
\code{poutlier} were zero, because the outlier component is a fixed regularizer
rather than a claim about how guesses are distributed. Use \code{\link[=with_outliers]{with_outliers()}}
for the fitted mixture - chiefly for \code{brms::pp_check()} - and
\code{\link[=without_outliers]{without_outliers()}} to go back. \code{log_lik()} is always the full mixture.

\code{posterior_epred()} is not provided: for a race model the expectation needs
numerical integration per draw and per observation, and users are better off
summarising \code{posterior_predict()} draws.
}

